6045443 true] [17592186672547 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(F-232)" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672547 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672547 1083 #uuid "24b00dff-c445-5900-a662-fe0a4bb25dac" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186672546 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672548 1071 "A wildlife manager noted that the growth of the number of deer in a protected forest is directly proportional to the difference between the forest’s maximum deer population and the current population. Let $N(t)$ be the deer population, measured in number of deer, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum deer population that the forest can support is $95$ deer, which differential equation could be used to model the forest’s deer population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672548 1072 #uuid "2667e815-917c-5f12-9c28-0d9309162349" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672548 1074 "The rate at which the deer population $N(t)$ of the forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum deer population the forest can support and the current deer population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum deer population the forest can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95\\text{ deer},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-N).\n$$" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186672571 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672549 1071 "A factory's management noted that the growth of the unit production is directly proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Let $P(t)$ be the production quantity, measured in units produced, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in days. If the maximum number of units that a worker can produce is $34$ $\\text{units}$, which differential equation could be used to model the factory's production? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672549 1072 #uuid "2be35662-b24d-5c89-874e-5c3591e8d395" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672549 1074 "The rate at which the production $P(t)$ of the factory changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce and the current production quantity. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of units a factory worker can produce, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=34\\text{ units},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(34-P).\n$$" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672550 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(P-34)" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672550 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672550 1083 #uuid "308cfbc3-831a-5173-a5c5-642d4518abf6" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186672575 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672551 1071 "An ecologist found that the growth of the number of frogs in a wetland is directly proportional to the difference between the wetland’s maximum frog population and the current population. Let $F(t)$ be the frog population, measured in number of frogs, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum frog population the wetland can support is $232$ frogs, which differential equation could be used to model the frog population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672551 1072 #uuid "322e7971-29aa-50c5-a6d3-ea7d6622a62d" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672551 1074 "The rate at which the frog population $F(t)$ of the wetland changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum frog population the wetland can support and the current frog population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-F),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum frog population the wetland can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=232\\text{ frogs},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(232-F).\n$$" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672552 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-k(34+P)" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672552 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672552 1083 #uuid "32deb58f-2a4b-5c2c-883c-3c0b907a77ef" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672553 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=k(P-156)" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672553 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672553 1083 #uuid "3f1baecc-44a1-5f72-ab09-9cd717b2aa17" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672554 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}=k(500-F)" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672554 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672554 1083 #uuid "434deb99-4c7c-5e2c-bba0-441d1fd87280" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672555 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(N-95)" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672555 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672555 1083 #uuid "4988d6b2-6cc8-58b8-937b-37187a3c0ccd" 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186672562 13194139538696 17592186045443 true] [17592186672556 1070 #e